ntially form:  \n- A Chow of T3-T4-T5 (one meld).  \n- A pair of B7 (since I have two B7).  \n- That leaves T7, B8, and F2 ungrouped, which are insufficient to form another meld.  \n\nI need one more tile to complete either a Chow with T7-B8 (requiring B9 or B6) or a pung with F2 (requiring two more F2), or other combinations. Therefore, I must discard a tile to improve my hand toward a winning shape.  \n\nPromising moves:  \n1. **Play T7**: T7 is isolated; discarding it keeps the T3-T4-T5 sequence intact and retains the B7 pair and B8 for a potential B6/B9 Chow.  \n2. **Play F2**: F2 is an honor tile with only one copy; discarding it simplifies the hand toward a suit-based hand, possibly aiming for All Simples or Half Flush.  \n3. **Play B8**: B8 is part of a potential Chow with B7; discarding it might be premature if I aim for a B6-B7-B8 Chow, but given I have two B7, keeping B8 could be useful if I draw B9.  \n\nReason: Discarding T7 seems best because it is the most isolated tile (no immediate neighbors in hand) and does not break any potential melds. Keeping F2 might allow for a Pung of Terminals or Honors if I draw more, but with only one F2 left in the unseen tiles (3 total, 1 in my hand, 1 discarded in history, so 1 left), it's unlikely. Discarding T7 maintains flexibility with the B7 pair and B8 for a Chow, and the T3-T4-T5 Chow is already set.  \n\nAnswer: Play T7"}
