honors/terminals. But it\u2019s also risky to discard terminals early. B7 is easier to discard because across player already used one in chow.  \n\nUnshown tile counts: W7 has 4 unseen, T3 has 3, T4 4, T5 4, B5 2, B7 3, B8 2. Discarding B5 or B8 leaves fewer in the pool, possibly giving others pung chances.  \n\nI should avoid discarding something that gives someone a pung or chow immediately. Next player\u2019s history shows lots of B9,B6,B3 plays, so they might be collecting B suits. But across player used B chows already. Previous player played B1,B8,B1,T9,W1 \u2014 seems they are discarding various suits.  \n\nBest safe discards: B8 (since across player already chowed with B8 and next player didn\u2019t claim B8 recently) or T4 (since T3,T2 were just discarded by across player).  \n\nFor tile efficiency: my hand has no obvious ready melds; keeping pairs helps, so keep B7 pair. Discard W7 (orphan terminal) or single B5/B8/T4/T5.  \n\n**Reason:**  \nB8 appears safer than B5 because B5 has only 2 left unseen and might complete someone\u2019s chow (B3\u2013B5 or B5\u2013B7). T4 is central but T3 and T5 are more likely to complete chows given T2 and T4/T6 in unseen tiles. W7 is terminal and no one\u2019s shown interest but many are left (4), so it\u2019s risky to keep in hopes of outside hand given my melds are T1 and W3 \u2014 not many terminals/honors.  \n\nI will choose **Play B8** as safest discard now to avoid giving away a tile that completes a chow or pung for opponents, while preserving my B7 pair and keeping flexibility in T3,T4,T5 for possible chow later.  \n\n**Answer:** Play B8"}
